  "notes": "West leads the ♠7 against 3NT. When East plays the ♠Q, it looks very natural to win the ♠A as South has a sure second stopper in the suit. However, if West has 5 spades to the ♠K and at least 1 of the ♦A or ♦K, the contract will be in serious danger of failing.\n\nEast will win the first defensive diamond trick and return a spade. West will win the ♠K and clear the suit. West will have 2 established spade tricks and an entry in diamonds. South will need a miracle in hearts to come to 9 tricks.\n\nSouth does better to duck East's ♠Q at trick 1. East will continue the suit, but North's holding still represents a second stopper. When East wins the first diamond trick he has no spades left to return.\n\nAfter regaining the lead, South can knock out the other diamond honour. West can only cash 1 spade trick and South has the balance.",
